{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "burden_type",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.95,
      "source_groundedness": 0.95,
      "rationale": "Categorising each ticket into a burden type is exactly the grouping axis needed to identify the 'most common operational burdens' and compare their resolution durations. Values are well-grounded readings of short_description (WiFi->Network, VPN->VPN, database->Database, printing->Hardware)."
    },
    {
      "name": "resolution_duration_minutes",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.55,
      "rationale": "Duration is the core outcome variable the query asks to quantify, so highly useful. However groundedness is questionable: opened_at is present but closed_at should drive duration, and the sample only shows opened_at/closed_by. The fractional values (24.5767, 47.9433) look precise but cannot be verified against the shown source, and no closed_at appears in the samples to confirm derivation."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_top_burden",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.4,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "rationale": "A flag marking whether a row belongs to a top burden is largely redundant with burden_type (derivable by comparing frequencies), adding little independent leverage. Also groundedness is inconsistent: Network_connectivity (80) and VPN (109) rows are marked 'no' while Database (136) is 'yes', suggesting an unclear/possibly incorrect threshold that isn't self-consistent with the top-value counts."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
  "source_groundedness": 0.68,
  "rationale": "burden_type provides the essential grouping axis and resolution_duration_minutes the essential outcome, together directly serving the query. is_top_burden is largely a restatement derivable from burden_type and adds little. Groundedness concerns: duration values are highly precise but their derivation from the shown source (no closed_at in samples) is unverified, and is_top_burden's yes/no assignment appears inconsistent with the observed frequency counts."
}

